  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef has a unique way of cutting round pizzas. He starts by cutting the pizza in half, then into quarters, then eighths, and so on, always cutting through the center.\n\nBelow is an example of cuts made by Chef in sequential order:\n\n\nChef wants to cut pizza into XXX slices where XXX is always even.\n\nYour task is to determine the number of pieces that will be **smaller** than the rest.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of single line of input, containing one even integer XXX — the number of slices Chef wants to cut.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the number of slices which are smaller than the others.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤X≤105;X2 \\leq X \\leq 10^5; X2≤X≤105;X is even"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2\n14\n100000",
        "output": "0\n12\n68928",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nRefer to the image in problem statement.\nTest case 111: There are two pizza slices in image (i). Both the slices are of equal size. Therefore, 000 slices are smaller than others.\nTest case 222: In image (vii), there are 141414 pizza slices, and 121212 of the slices are smaller than the other 222."
      }
    ]
  },
